3. TOP 5 DEEP DIVES #1 Jevlike =E2=80=94 TEST NOW

This is the closest concrete open implementation yet found to the JEV
mechanism: context + variable option set =E2=86=92 probability distribution=
,
without prose generation. More interestingly, it extends the same
option-attention head into visual action selection for Doom/chess. That
makes JEV less like a niche API format and more like a candidate generic
fast-control primitive.

Privately experiment: put Jevlike between an LLM planner and QP. Give it
5-20 bounded actions such as ALLOW / DENY / ESCALATE / SANDBOX /
REQUIRE_PROOF and compare latency, calibration and policy consistency
against an LLM JSON classifier.
Falsifier: no meaningful latency/cost/reliability advantage after
calibration on real Privately decisions.
#2 Native Agent Runtime Router =E2=80=94 ADOPT/COMPARE

NAR is unusually aligned with the existing QP/atask direction:
deterministic model-free kernel, bounded waiting, workspace locking,
verification, one repair, cancellation confirmation, budgets, recovery,
usage accounting and per-agent scoring. This is much closer to a reusable
autonomy kernel than another chat-agent framework.

Experiment: map NAR=E2=80=99s run/wait/inspect/cancel contract against QP
task/proof/budget objects. Identify primitives to copy rather than
integrating wholesale.
#3 Pipelock =E2=80=94 ADOPT/BAKEOFF

Pipelock is moving beyond a generic MCP firewall toward an external
evidence-producing mediator: it scans bidirectional traffic and emits
signed receipts from outside the agent. This directly overlaps Privately=E2=
=80=99s
thesis that evidence should be produced at the authority boundary, not
self-reported by the agent.

Experiment: run identical benign exfiltration/rug-pull/tool-chain scenarios
through Pipelock, Veil MCP and QP prototype. Compare observable evidence,
false positives, latency and policy expressiveness.
#4 Recuris =E2=80=94 BUILD PROBE

Recuris=E2=80=99s key distinction is not merely =E2=80=98agent memory=E2=80=
=99: it decomposes skill
memory, uses structured trajectories to locate failure in a component,
patches only that component, then uses deterministic held-out validation to
decide survival. This is extremely close to the desired QP
processor-evolution model.

Experiment: represent one QP processor=E2=80=99s rules as evolvable memory =
and
require paired held-out proof before promotion.
#5 SkillCloak/SkillDetonate =E2=80=94 CONTENT + LAB

The durable lesson is that install-time appearance is not enough. The
research reports >90% bypass for its packing approach across tested static
scanners, while runtime SkillDetonate tracks materialized instructions and
sensitive-data flows. This reinforces the scanner=E2=80=99s earlier
TEMPORAL_TRUST_DRIFT finding from a different direction: authority should
attach to observed behaviour and continuing identity, not merely the
artifact reviewed at install time.
